🚽 Test case: slorp<T>(T) -> T is identity-polymorphic on its argument's
🚽 runtime type. For a char array (`yap buf[32]`), the source-level
🚽 VarType is VAR_CHAR, but ast_expr_to_stdrot_value() marshals it as
🚽 STDROT_STRING (its own VAR_CHAR/is_array branch), and slorp's own
🚽 dispatch returns STDROT_STRING right back -- so `rant s = slorp(buf);`
🚽 must type-check statically (infer_expression_abi_type(), not plain
🚽 infer_expression_type(), for the return_like_arg identity inference)
🚽 and actually produce a real string at runtime.
skibidi main {
    yap buf[32];
    rant s = slorp(buf);
    yapping("%s", s);
    bussin 0;
}
